STRATEGY DESIGN PATTERN
INTERVIEW NOTES (CLEAN & FINAL)
=========================================================
Strategy is a behavioral design pattern that defines a family of algorithms, 
encapsulates each algorithm into a separate class, and makes them interchangeable so that the algorithm can be selected at runtime.

Start with the problem:

"Suppose I'm implementing a payment system that supports Credit Card, UPI, and PayPal. 
A straightforward implementation might use a large if-else or switch statement."

"This has several problems:

Every time a new payment method is added, I must modify existing code.
The class becomes large and hard to maintain.
It violates the Open/Closed Principle.
Testing each payment algorithm independently becomes difficult."

Then explain the solution

"The Strategy pattern solves this by moving each algorithm into its own class. 
All payment methods implement a common interface, and the context holds a reference or pointer to that interface."

Explain the runtime behavior

"The client creates the required strategy and passes it to the PaymentProcessor. 
The processor doesn't know whether it's using Credit Card, UPI, or PayPal—it simply calls strategy->pay(). 
Because it depends only on the interface, new payment methods can be added without modifying the processor."




1. What is Strategy Pattern?
----------------------------
Strategy is a Behavioral Design Pattern.

It defines a family of algorithms, encapsulates each algorithm into a separate
class, and makes them interchangeable at runtime.

Definition:
"Encapsulate interchangeable algorithms and choose the required one at runtime."

---------------------------------------------------------

2. Why do we need Strategy Pattern?
-----------------------------------

Problem without Strategy:

Suppose we have a Payment class:

class Payment
{
public:
    void pay(string type)
    {
        if(type == "CreditCard")
            ...
        else if(type == "UPI")
            ...
        else if(type == "PayPal")
            ...
    }
};

Problems:

❌ Large if-else / switch statements
❌ Adding a new algorithm requires modifying existing code
❌ Violates Open/Closed Principle (OCP)
❌ Difficult to test individual algorithms
❌ Poor maintainability
❌ Tight coupling between client and implementations

---------------------------------------------------------

3. Solution
-----------

Move each algorithm into its own class.

Client chooses the required algorithm at runtime.

                Strategy
                   ▲
                   │
        ------------------------
        │          │           │
      UPI      CreditCard    PayPal

Context
   |
uses Strategy

